<main class="container">

  <!-- S1: THE CONNECTION -->
  <section class="section" id="connection">
    <div class="section-tag">The Kidney–Brain Axis</div>
    <h2>
      <span data-lang="en">Why CKD Affects Your Mind</span>
      <span data-lang="tl" class="lang-hidden">Bakit Nakaka-apekto ang CKD sa Inyong Isipan</span>
      <span data-lang="ceb" class="lang-hidden">Ngano ang CKD Nakaapekto sa Imong Hunahuna</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="intro-callout">
      <p>Depression, anxiety, poor sleep, and cognitive difficulties are not just reactions to difficult news — they are <strong>direct biological consequences of kidney disease.</strong> Uremic toxins, hormonal disruption, anemia, inflammation, and dialysis itself all alter brain chemistry. Treating your mental health and sleep is treating your kidney disease.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="num-grid">
      <div class="num-card"><div class="n-val">20–40%</div><div class="n-label">of CKD patients have clinically significant depression</div><div class="n-target">vs ~7% in the general population</div></div>
      <div class="num-card"><div class="n-val">~50%</div><div class="n-label">of dialysis patients report significant sleep disturbance</div><div class="n-target">Most go undiagnosed and untreated</div></div>
      <div class="num-card"><div class="n-val">25–30%</div><div class="n-label">of CKD patients have restless legs syndrome (RLS)</div><div class="n-target">10× higher than general population</div></div>
      <div class="num-card"><div class="n-val">2–3×</div><div class="n-label">higher mortality risk when depression goes untreated in CKD</div><div class="n-target">Mental health treatment saves lives</div></div>
    </div>

    <h3>How CKD Harms the Brain — The Biology</h3>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-red"></span>Uremic Toxins</h4>
        <p>As kidneys fail to filter, toxins like indoxyl sulfate and p-cresol cross the blood-brain barrier, disrupting neurotransmitter function and triggering neuroinflammation — directly causing cognitive decline, fatigue, and mood disturbance.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-amber"></span>Anemia &amp; Brain Hypoxia</h4>
        <p>Reduced erythropoietin from damaged kidneys causes anemia. The brain is acutely sensitive to oxygen deprivation — even mild anemia (Hgb 9–10 g/dL) causes fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, and depressed mood.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-navy"></span>Chronic Inflammation</h4>
        <p>CKD drives persistent elevation of IL-6, TNF-α, and CRP. These inflammatory cytokines directly suppress serotonin and dopamine synthesis — the neurotransmitters that regulate mood, motivation, and pleasure.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-teal"></span>Hormonal Disruption</h4>
        <p>Disrupted calcium, phosphorus, and PTH alter nerve excitability and sleep architecture. Reduced testosterone and thyroid function — common in CKD — further compound fatigue, depression, and sexual dysfunction.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-purple"></span>The Burden of Illness</h4>
        <p>Dietary restrictions, fluid limits, multiple medications, frequent clinic visits, fear of progression, financial stress, and loss of independence all create a chronic psychological burden that is real, valid, and treatable.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-green"></span>The Sleep–Mood Cycle</h4>
        <p>Poor sleep worsens depression. Depression worsens sleep. In CKD, both are amplified by uremic pruritus, restless legs, nocturia, fluid shifts overnight, and dialysis-day schedule disruption — a self-reinforcing cycle.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- S2: DEPRESSION -->
  <section class="section" id="depression">
    <div class="section-tag">Mental Health</div>
    <h2>
      <span data-lang="en">Depression in CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="tl" class="lang-hidden">Depresyon sa CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="ceb" class="lang-hidden">Depresyon sa CKD</span>
    </h2>
    <p>Depression in CKD is not weakness, self-pity, or a character flaw. It is a medical condition with biological drivers — and it is undertreated in up to 75% of affected patients. Untreated depression in CKD is independently associated with faster kidney disease progression, higher hospitalization rates, and premature death.</p>

    <div class="alert alert-purple">
      <div class="alert-icon">🧠</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>Recognizing Depression — Common Symptoms in CKD Patients</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Persistent sadness, emptiness, or hopelessness lasting more than 2 weeks</li>
          <li>Loss of interest in activities you previously enjoyed</li>
          <li>Fatigue disproportionate to your level of physical activity (beyond what uremia explains)</li>
          <li>Changes in appetite or weight not explained by dietary restrictions</li>
          <li>Difficulty concentrating, making decisions, or remembering</li>
          <li>Feeling worthless, guilty, or like a burden to your family</li>
          <li>Thoughts of death or of not wanting to continue treatment</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert alert-red">
      <div class="alert-icon">🆘</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>If You Are Having Thoughts of Suicide or Self-Harm — Tell Someone Now</h4>
        <p>These thoughts are a medical emergency, not a character failing. Tell your nephrologist, a nurse, a family member, or call the <strong>National Center for Mental Health Crisis Hotline: 1553</strong> (24/7, Philippines). You are not alone, and you deserve help immediately.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Treatment of Depression in CKD</h3>
    <table class="lab-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Treatment</th><th>Evidence in CKD</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)</td><td><span class="target-badge">Strong — first-line</span></td><td>Effective and kidney-safe. In-person or via telehealth. Recommended before or alongside medications.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SSRIs — sertraline, escitalopram</td><td><span class="target-badge">Good — preferred class</span></td><td>Most renal safety data for these two. May need dose adjustment at eGFR &lt;30. Avoid paroxetine (high anticholinergic burden).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SNRIs — duloxetine</td><td><span class="caution-badge">Use with caution</span></td><td>Accumulates in severe CKD/ESKD — avoid at eGFR &lt;30. Useful for co-existing painful neuropathy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Regular aerobic exercise</td><td><span class="target-badge">Strong evidence</span></td><td>30 min moderate exercise ≥3×/week reduces depressive symptoms comparably to SSRIs in mild-moderate depression. Completely kidney-safe.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tricyclics (TCAs)</td><td><span class="avoid-badge">Generally avoid</span></td><td>High anticholinergic and cardiotoxic risk. Active metabolites accumulate in CKD. Not recommended as antidepressants.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mirtazapine</td><td><span class="caution-badge">Use with caution</span></td><td>Low doses (7.5–15 mg at bedtime) may help depression, insomnia, and appetite simultaneously in CKD. Discuss with your doctor.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- S3: ANXIETY -->
  <section class="section" id="anxiety">
    <div class="section-tag">Mental Health</div>
    <h2>
      <span data-lang="en">Anxiety &amp; CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="tl" class="lang-hidden">Pagkabalisa at CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="ceb" class="lang-hidden">Kahadlok ug CKD</span>
    </h2>
    <p>Anxiety affects 19–45% of CKD patients and frequently co-exists with depression. The fears are often legitimate — dialysis dependence, transplant uncertainty, financial burden, and shortened life expectancy are genuinely threatening. Anxiety becomes a medical problem when it is persistent, out of proportion, and interfering with daily functioning and treatment adherence.</p>

    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-amber"></span>Health Anxiety</h4>
        <p>Excessive worry about every lab result, every symptom, every missed dose. Can lead to avoidance of testing (fear of bad news) or the opposite — over-medicalization and frequent ER visits for non-urgent concerns.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-red"></span>Dialysis-Related Anxiety</h4>
        <p>Needle phobia, fear of access complications, anxiety during sessions, and inter-dialysis anxiety (the 48–72 hour window with fluid and toxin accumulation). Very common and very treatable.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-navy"></span>Anticipatory Grief</h4>
        <p>Grieving losses that have not yet occurred — anticipated loss of kidney function, independence, or life expectancy. Discussing this openly with your doctor and loved ones often reduces its power significantly.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert alert-teal">
      <div class="alert-icon">🧘</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>Evidence-Based Non-Drug Approaches for Anxiety in CKD</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Diaphragmatic breathing:</strong> The 4–7–8 technique (inhale 4 sec, hold 7, exhale 8) activates the parasympathetic nervous system within 90 seconds. Can be practiced during dialysis sessions.</li>
          <li><strong>CBT and Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR):</strong> Multiple RCTs show significant anxiety reduction in dialysis patients. Ask for a referral.</li>
          <li><strong>Peer support:</strong> Connecting with other CKD patients who have navigated the same fears normalizes the experience and reduces isolation dramatically.</li>
          <li><strong>Scheduled worry time:</strong> Containing anxiety to a designated 20-minute daily window prevents it from colonizing the entire day — a validated CBT technique.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- S4: BRAIN FOG -->
  <section class="section" id="cognitive">
    <div class="section-tag">Cognitive Health</div>
    <h2>
      <span data-lang="en">Brain Fog &amp; Cognitive Impairment in CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="tl" class="lang-hidden">Brain Fog at Pagbaba ng Kakayahan sa Pag-iisip sa CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="ceb" class="lang-hidden">Brain Fog ug Pagkubos sa Pag-iisip sa CKD</span>
    </h2>
    <p>Brain fog — difficulty with memory, word-finding, concentration, and processing speed — affects up to 60% of CKD patients and is present even in early stages. It is not aging, not imaginary, and not always irreversible. In many patients, treating the underlying biological drivers significantly improves cognitive function.</p>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-red"></span>Treatable Causes to Investigate First</h4>
        <p>Anemia (Hgb &lt;10 g/dL), poor dialysis adequacy (spKt/V &lt;1.4), uncontrolled hypertension, hypoglycemia episodes, sleep apnea, thyroid dysfunction, and B12 or folate deficiency all cause cognitive impairment and are reversible with treatment.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-teal"></span>Strategies to Protect Brain Function</h4>
        <p>Optimal BP control (&lt;130/80 mmHg), adequate dialysis, anemia correction, regular aerobic exercise, quality sleep, social engagement, and cognitively stimulating activities (reading, puzzles, conversation) all measurably preserve and improve brain function in CKD.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert alert-amber">
      <div class="alert-icon">⚠️</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>Brain Fog Affects Medication Adherence — Tell Your Doctor</h4>
        <p>If cognitive impairment is making it hard to remember medications, dosing times, or dietary restrictions, disclose this to your doctor and pharmacist. Pill organizers, phone alarms, simplified once-daily regimens, blister packs, and caregiver involvement are legitimate and important medical interventions — not signs of giving up.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- S5: SLEEP AND CKD -->
  <section class="section" id="sleep">
    <div class="section-tag">Sleep Medicine</div>
    <h2>
      <span data-lang="en">Sleep &amp; CKD — Why It's So Difficult</span>
      <span data-lang="tl" class="lang-hidden">Tulog at CKD — Bakit Ito ay Napakahirap</span>
      <span data-lang="ceb" class="lang-hidden">Pagtulog ug CKD — Ngano Kini Lisod Kaayo</span>
    </h2>
    <p>Good sleep is when the brain consolidates memory, the body repairs tissue, the immune system resets, and blood pressure drops to its nocturnal nadir. In CKD, virtually every biological and logistical barrier to sleep is present simultaneously.</p>

    <div class="num-grid">
      <div class="num-card"><div class="n-val">6.5 hr</div><div class="n-label">Average sleep duration in dialysis patients</div><div class="n-target">vs 7–9 hr recommended</div></div>
      <div class="num-card"><div class="n-val">50–80%</div><div class="n-label">of hemodialysis patients report insomnia symptoms</div><div class="n-target">Most never report it to their doctor</div></div>
      <div class="num-card"><div class="n-val">30–73%</div><div class="n-label">of ESKD patients have obstructive sleep apnea</div><div class="n-target">Dramatically worsens CV risk</div></div>
    </div>

    <h3>Why CKD Disrupts Sleep — The Mechanisms</h3>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-red"></span>Uremic Pruritus (Itching)</h4>
        <p>Uremic toxins deposited in the skin activate nerve fibers and trigger persistent itching — worst at night. Affects up to 40% of dialysis patients and is one of the most common causes of sleep disruption. Difelikefalin (Kapruvia) is now approved for this indication.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-amber"></span>Nocturia</h4>
        <p>As kidneys lose concentrating ability, urine production continues or increases at night. Patients wake 2–5 times per night to urinate, preventing deep sleep stages. Fluid redistribution from dependent edema during recumbency worsens this.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-navy"></span>Dysregulated Melatonin</h4>
        <p>CKD disrupts circadian melatonin secretion — the hormone that signals nighttime to the brain. Dialysis itself blunts the melatonin nocturnal surge. This is why low-dose melatonin (0.5–3 mg) often helps CKD-related insomnia.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-teal"></span>Fluid Overload</h4>
        <p>Excess fluid volume causes orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat), pulmonary congestion, and redistribution to the upper airway overnight — worsening sleep apnea and causing repetitive arousals.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-purple"></span>Pain &amp; Cramping</h4>
        <p>Dialysis-associated muscle cramps (from electrolyte shifts), peripheral neuropathy pain, and bone pain from CKD-MBD are all peak at night and severely fragment sleep architecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-green"></span>Dialysis Schedule</h4>
        <p>Early morning dialysis start times, post-dialysis fatigue, and the physiological stress of fluid and solute removal all disrupt normal sleep-wake cycles — particularly for patients on in-center HD 3× weekly.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- S6: RESTLESS LEGS -->
  <section class="section" id="rls">
    <div class="section-tag">Sleep Disorder</div>
    <h2>
      <span data-lang="en">Restless Legs Syndrome (RLS) in CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="tl" class="lang-hidden">Restless Legs Syndrome (RLS) sa CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="ceb" class="lang-hidden">Restless Legs Syndrome (RLS) sa CKD</span>
    </h2>
    <p>Restless Legs Syndrome — an irresistible urge to move the legs, typically worse at rest and at night — is 10 times more common in CKD than in the general population. It is caused by dopaminergic dysfunction compounded by iron deficiency, peripheral neuropathy, and uremic toxin accumulation. It is frequently misdiagnosed as anxiety, cramps, or "just nerves."</p>

    <div class="alert alert-teal">
      <div class="alert-icon">🦵</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>Diagnosing RLS — The 4 Essential Features (All Must Be Present)</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>An urge to move the legs, usually accompanied by uncomfortable sensations (crawling, tingling, burning, aching)</li>
          <li>Symptoms begin or worsen during periods of rest or inactivity</li>
          <li>Symptoms are partially or fully relieved by movement (walking, stretching)</li>
          <li>Symptoms are worse in the evening or night, and better in the morning</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Treatment of RLS in CKD</h3>
    <table class="lab-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Intervention</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Notes for CKD</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Iron repletion (if deficient)</td><td><span class="target-badge">First-line</span></td><td>IV iron sucrose (Ferrofer / Anema) preferred in dialysis patients. Target ferritin &gt;200, TSAT &gt;30%. Corrects both RLS and anemia simultaneously.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Optimize dialysis adequacy</td><td><span class="target-badge">First-line</span></td><td>spKt/V ≥1.4. Longer or more frequent dialysis sessions significantly reduce RLS severity. Longer dialysis time clears uremic toxins driving dopaminergic dysfunction.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Low-dose dopamine agonists — pramipexole, ropinirole</td><td><span class="target-badge">Effective</span></td><td>Use lowest effective dose. Risk of augmentation (symptom worsening over time) with long-term use — start only after iron and dialysis optimization. Dose-reduce at lower eGFR.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gabapentin / pregabalin</td><td><span class="target-badge">Effective in CKD</span></td><td>Preferred over dopamine agonists if neuropathic pain co-exists. <strong>Dose must be significantly reduced</strong> based on eGFR — gabapentin accumulates in CKD and can cause sedation, falls, and encephalopathy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Clonazepam (low dose)</td><td><span class="caution-badge">Use with caution</span></td><td>Reduces nighttime awakenings from RLS. Risk of sedation, falls, and dependence — short courses only. Avoid in elderly CKD patients.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stretching, warm bath before bed</td><td><span class="target-badge">Simple, safe</span></td><td>Calf stretching, moderate leg exercise in the afternoon, and a warm bath 1–2 hr before bedtime reliably reduce RLS symptom severity in most patients.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- S7: SLEEP APNEA -->
  <section class="section" id="apnea">
    <div class="section-tag">Sleep Disorder</div>
    <h2>
      <span data-lang="en">Sleep Apnea &amp; CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="tl" class="lang-hidden">Sleep Apnea at CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="ceb" class="lang-hidden">Sleep Apnea ug CKD</span>
    </h2>
    <p>Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) — repeated collapse of the upper airway during sleep causing oxygen desaturation and arousal — affects 30–73% of patients with ESKD. This is 3–4× higher than the general population. In CKD, OSA is driven by fluid redistribution to the neck during recumbency, uremic edema of the upper airway, and altered chemoreceptor sensitivity.</p>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-red"></span>Why OSA in CKD Matters Urgently</h4>
        <p>Untreated OSA in CKD accelerates kidney disease progression (nocturnal hypoxia directly injures tubular cells), worsens hypertension (surges with each arousal), drives arrhythmias, and significantly increases cardiovascular mortality — the leading cause of death in dialysis patients.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-teal"></span>Treatment: CPAP is Kidney-Protective</h4>
        <p>Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) therapy in dialysis patients with OSA reduces nocturnal hypertension, improves eGFR slope in pre-dialysis CKD, and significantly improves daytime fatigue, mood, and cognitive function. It is one of the most impactful treatable comorbidities in CKD.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert alert-amber">
      <div class="alert-icon">😴</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>Warning Signs of OSA — Ask Your Doctor for a Sleep Study</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Loud, witnessed snoring — especially with pauses in breathing observed by a bed partner</li>
          <li>Waking unrefreshed despite sufficient hours of sleep</li>
          <li>Daytime sleepiness that interferes with daily activities, driving, or work</li>
          <li>Morning headaches (from overnight CO₂ retention)</li>
          <li>Difficulty controlling blood pressure despite multiple medications</li>
          <li>New or worsening cardiac arrhythmias</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- S8: MEDICATIONS AND SLEEP -->
  <section class="section" id="meds">
    <div class="section-tag">Pharmacology</div>
    <h2>
      <span data-lang="en">Medications That Affect Sleep in CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="tl" class="lang-hidden">Mga Gamot na Nakaka-apekto sa Tulog sa CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="ceb" class="lang-hidden">Mga Tambal nga Nakaapekto sa Pagtulog sa CKD</span>
    </h2>
    <p>Many medications that CKD patients take for entirely appropriate reasons can significantly worsen sleep. Recognizing this allows your doctor to adjust timing, dose, or substitute with a sleep-friendlier alternative.</p>

    <table class="lab-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Medication / Class</th><th>Effect on Sleep</th><th>What to Do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Diuretics (furosemide, torsemide)</td><td>Nocturia if dosed in the evening</td><td>Take in the morning or by 2 PM. Avoids overnight diuresis and sleep fragmentation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Beta-blockers (carvedilol, metoprolol)</td><td>Nightmares, vivid dreams, insomnia — especially lipophilic agents</td><td>Discuss switching to atenolol or bisoprolol (less lipophilic, fewer CNS effects) if sleep is severely affected.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Corticosteroids (prednisone)</td><td>Insomnia, difficulty falling asleep — peak effect 4–6 hr after dose</td><td>Take in the morning with food whenever possible. Avoid evening dosing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus)</td><td>Insomnia, tremor, anxiety — especially at high trough levels</td><td>Check trough level. Sleep improvement may signal over-immunosuppression needing dose adjustment.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Phosphate binders (calcium carbonate)</td><td>Constipation, bloating, abdominal discomfort → difficulty lying flat</td><td>Take with meals only. Ensure adequate hydration within fluid limits.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Iron supplements (oral)</td><td>GI upset, cramping, nausea → difficulty falling asleep</td><td>Switch to evening dose with food, or discuss IV iron sucrose (Ferrofer/Anema) to bypass GI entirely.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gabapentin / pregabalin</td><td>Can improve sleep at appropriate doses; causes sedation and falls at excessive doses in CKD</td><td>Must be dose-adjusted for eGFR. <strong>Do not use standard general-population doses</strong> — accumulation risk is high in CKD.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Benzodiazepines (diazepam, alprazolam)</td><td>Short-term sleep induction; suppress deep sleep stages; dependency risk; accumulate in CKD</td><td>Avoid for chronic insomnia. If used at all — shortest course, lowest dose, with exit plan. Fall risk is significant.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Sleep Aids That Are Relatively Safe in CKD</h3>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-green"></span>Melatonin (0.5–3 mg)</h4>
        <p>Low-dose melatonin taken 30–60 minutes before target sleep time corrects the disrupted circadian signaling of CKD. Multiple small RCTs show improved sleep quality in dialysis patients. No renal toxicity. Start low (0.5 mg) and titrate.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-teal"></span>Doxylamine (low dose)</h4>
        <p>An antihistamine sedative (active ingredient in Unisom). Short courses may help. Use with caution in elderly CKD patients due to anticholinergic effects (urinary retention, confusion). Not for nightly use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-amber"></span>Mirtazapine (7.5 mg at bedtime)</h4>
        <p>Low-dose mirtazapine improves sleep, appetite, and depression simultaneously in CKD — useful when all three co-exist. Sedating at low doses. Discuss with your nephrologist before starting.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert alert-red">
      <div class="alert-icon">🚫</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>Medications to Avoid for Sleep in CKD</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Zolpidem (Ambien) and related z-drugs:</strong> Accumulate in CKD, cause next-day sedation, falls, and paradoxical agitation. High fall and fracture risk in CKD patients who already have bone disease.</li>
          <li><strong>Diphenhydramine (in OTC sleep aids):</strong> Strong anticholinergic effects — urinary retention, constipation, confusion in CKD patients. Avoid, especially in elderly.</li>
          <li><strong>Chloral hydrate:</strong> Accumulates in renal failure. Avoid completely.</li>
          <li><strong>Herbal "sleep teas" without physician review:</strong> Valerian, kava, and some traditional herbal preparations have nephrotoxic potential. Always disclose any supplement to your doctor.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- S9: SLEEP HYGIENE -->
  <section class="section" id="hygiene">
    <div class="section-tag">Sleep Strategies</div>
    <h2>
      <span data-lang="en">Sleep Hygiene for CKD Patients</span>
      <span data-lang="tl" class="lang-hidden">Sleep Hygiene para sa mga Pasyenteng CKD</span>
      <span data-lang="ceb" class="lang-hidden">Sleep Hygiene alang sa mga Pasyente nga CKD</span>
    </h2>
    <p>Sleep hygiene refers to the behaviors and environment that support consistent, restorative sleep. In CKD, standard recommendations need modification to account for fluid restrictions, nocturia, RLS, and dialysis schedules.</p>

    <div class="sleep-block">
      <h3>CKD-Adapted Sleep Hygiene — 10 Evidence-Based Rules</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Consistent sleep and wake times:</strong> Even on dialysis days. Irregular schedules destroy circadian rhythms faster than almost anything else.</li>
        <li><strong>Front-load fluids before 6 PM:</strong> Take most of your daily fluid allowance in the morning and afternoon. Minimize intake after dinner to reduce nocturia.</li>
        <li><strong>Leg elevation for 1–2 hours before bed:</strong> Reduces fluid redistribution from legs to neck overnight — directly reduces both nocturia frequency and OSA severity.</li>
        <li><strong>No screens 60 minutes before bed:</strong> Blue light suppresses melatonin. In CKD, where melatonin secretion is already impaired, this amplification is especially harmful.</li>
        <li><strong>Cool, dark, quiet bedroom (18–20°C):</strong> Core body temperature must drop for sleep onset. In CKD, thermoregulation is impaired — a cool room supports this drop.</li>
        <li><strong>Afternoon exercise — not within 3 hours of bedtime:</strong> Exercise reduces RLS severity when done 4–6 hours before bed. Late evening exercise is activating and delays sleep onset.</li>
        <li><strong>Calf stretching routine before bed (10 minutes):</strong> Stretch gastrocnemius and soleus muscles — reliably reduces RLS and nocturnal cramping severity.</li>
        <li><strong>No naps longer than 20 minutes after 3 PM:</strong> Long naps reduce sleep pressure and make night sleep harder. Short "power naps" (15–20 min) do not interfere.</li>
        <li><strong>Stimulus control:</strong> Use the bed only for sleep and intimacy. If you cannot sleep after 20 minutes, get up, do something quiet in dim light, and return only when sleepy. This retrains the brain-bed association.</li>
        <li><strong>Warm shower or bath 1–2 hours before bed:</strong> The post-bath drop in core temperature mimics the natural pre-sleep temperature drop and reliably hastens sleep onset — also reduces RLS symptoms.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="alert alert-teal">
      <div class="alert-icon">💡</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Insomnia (CBT-I) — The Gold Standard</h4>
        <p>CBT-I is more effective than sleeping pills for chronic insomnia — including in CKD patients — with benefits that persist long after therapy ends. It includes sleep restriction therapy, stimulus control, cognitive restructuring of sleep-related worry, and relaxation training. Ask your nephrologist for a referral to a sleep psychologist or CBT-I therapist. Telehealth delivery is widely available.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- S10: GETTING HELP -->
  <section class="section" id="support">
    <div class="section-tag">Care Team</div>
    <h2>
      <span data-lang="en">Getting Help — Who to Talk To</span>
      <span data-lang="tl" class="lang-hidden">Paghingi ng Tulong — Kanino Makikipag-usap</span>
      <span data-lang="ceb" class="lang-hidden">Pagpangayo og Tabang — Kinsa ang Imong Kaugnayan</span>
    </h2>
    <p>Mental health and sleep problems in CKD are medical problems — not personal failures. A skilled care team can address them systematically. You do not need to suffer in silence, and you do not need to figure this out alone.</p>

    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-body"><h4>Your Nephrologist — First Stop</h4><p>Your nephrologist can screen for depression and anxiety using validated tools (PHQ-9, GAD-7), order a sleep study if OSA is suspected, adjust medications contributing to sleep problems, optimize dialysis adequacy and anemia — and provide referrals. Bring up mental health and sleep at every visit. These are not separate from your kidney care — they are part of it.</p></div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-body"><h4>Renal Social Worker</h4><p>Every dialysis unit should have a renal social worker. They can provide counseling, connect you to community resources, address financial and insurance burdens that drive anxiety, facilitate family communication, and coordinate mental health referrals. If you don't know yours, ask.</p></div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-body"><h4>Psychiatrist or Psychologist</h4><p>For moderate-to-severe depression, anxiety, or refractory insomnia, a mental health professional with experience in medically ill patients is invaluable. Psychiatric medications require careful renal dose adjustment — a psychiatrist familiar with CKD is the ideal prescriber.</p></div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-body"><h4>Sleep Medicine Specialist</h4><p>For suspected OSA, refractory insomnia, or severe RLS, a formal sleep study (polysomnography or home sleep apnea test) and evaluation by a sleep medicine specialist provides the definitive diagnosis and treatment plan. CPAP titration is done through this pathway.</p></div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-body"><h4>Peer Support &amp; Patient Organizations</h4><p>The National Kidney Foundation and the Philippine Society of Nephrology both maintain patient networks. Connecting with other CKD patients who are successfully managing mental health and sleep challenges provides perspective, practical advice, and the irreplaceable knowledge that you are not alone.</p></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert alert-purple">
      <div class="alert-icon">💬</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>How to Start the Conversation With Your Doctor</h4>
        <p>Many patients feel embarrassed to mention mental health or sleep at a nephrology visit — worried it will seem unrelated or take up too much time. Use these direct openers: <em>"My sleep has been very poor and I think it is affecting my overall health — can we address this?"</em> or <em>"I have been feeling depressed and anxious more days than not. Is that something we can discuss and treat?"</em> These are medical symptoms. Your doctor needs to know.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- S11: QUESTIONS -->
  <section class="section" id="questions">
    <div class="section-tag">Your Next Appointment</div>
    <h2>
      <span data-lang="en">Questions to Ask Your Doctor</span>
      <span data-lang="tl" class="lang-hidden">Mga Tanong na Itatanong sa Inyong Doktor</span>
      <span data-lang="ceb" class="lang-hidden">Mga Pangutana nga Itatanong sa Imong Doktor</span>
    </h2>
    <p>Bring these questions to your next visit. Being specific helps your doctor help you more efficiently.</p>

    <div class="checklist">
      <h4>My Questions for My Doctor</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Can you screen me for depression and anxiety using a questionnaire today?</li>
        <li>I have been sleeping poorly — can we figure out why? Should I be evaluated for sleep apnea or RLS?</li>
        <li>Could any of my current medications be worsening my sleep or mood?</li>
        <li>Is my anemia well-controlled? Could low hemoglobin be contributing to my fatigue and brain fog?</li>
        <li>Is my dialysis dose adequate? Could poor clearance be making me feel mentally worse?</li>
        <li>Is low-dose melatonin something I can try for my sleep problems?</li>
        <li>Can you refer me to a sleep specialist or a mental health professional who has experience with CKD patients?</li>
        <li>Can I meet with the renal social worker to discuss the psychological and financial burden I am experiencing?</li>
        <li>Are there any peer support groups or patient communities for CKD mental health that you recommend?</li>
        <li>If I need an antidepressant, which one is safest given my current eGFR and medications?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="disclaimer">
      <strong>Important:</strong> This guide is for educational purposes only. It does not replace individualized medical advice, diagnosis, or psychiatric assessment. If you are experiencing a mental health crisis or thoughts of self-harm, contact the <strong>NCMH Crisis Hotline: 1553</strong> immediately. All medication decisions — including sleep aids and antidepressants in CKD — require physician review and renal dose adjustment.
    </div>

    <div class="dr-card">
      <div style="width:64px;height:64px;border-radius:50%;overflow:hidden;flex-shrink:0;border:2px solid rgba(184,150,46,0.5);background:#0f1e2e;">
        <img src="../images/photo-contact.jpg" alt="Dr. W. G. M. Rivero" style="width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover;object-position:center top;">
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4><a href="https://williamriveromd.com" style="color:white;text-decoration:none;">W. G. M. Rivero, MD, FPCP, DPSN</a></h4>
